        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Getting rid of a treadmill comes down to one question asked first: does it work? A working
          machine has a value and a queue of people who will collect it, and the cheapest disposal
          is the one where somebody else does the lifting. A broken one is scrap steel, a motor, a
          circuit board and a belt, and the job is getting each to somewhere that will take it.
          Either way, there is a little preparation that makes the whole thing faster and safer.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Dispose of a Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            If it works, <strong>sell it or give it away</strong> to someone who will collect it —
            that is usually the cheapest and least effort, and a working treadmill finds a taker
            quickly. If you are buying a new one, <strong>ask the retailer about haul-away</strong>
            of the old machine at delivery. If it does not work, check your <strong>municipal
            bulk-item pickup</strong> or local transfer station, take it to a <strong>scrap metal
            recycler</strong>, or pay a <strong>junk removal service</strong> to carry it out,
            which typically costs somewhere in the range of $75 to $250.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before any of that: log out of any connected fitness account on the console and reset
            it, remove the safety key, and decide how the machine is getting out of the room. A
            treadmill is a two-person lift at minimum, and the person collecting it will want to
            know about stairs before they arrive.
          </p>
          <p>
            Charities are worth a phone call but not an assumption. Many donation centres do not
            accept large fitness equipment at all, and those that do usually want it working and
            sometimes want it delivered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="options-at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Your Options at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            Roughly in order of preference for a working machine, then for a broken one.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sell it</strong> — best for a working machine less than about eight years
            old. You get money, the buyer does the collecting. Costs you photographs and some
            messages.</li>
            <li><strong>Give it away free</strong> — fastest route for a working but older or
            unremarkable machine. Free listings usually go within days.</li>
            <li><strong>Donate to a charity or community group</strong> — worthwhile if one near
            you accepts fitness equipment. Call first.</li>
            <li><strong>Retailer haul-away</strong> — the easiest option if you are replacing the
            machine. Sometimes free, often a fee, not always offered.</li>
            <li><strong>Municipal bulk pickup or drop-off</strong> — cheap or free for a broken
            machine, subject to local rules about items with motors and electronics.</li>
            <li><strong>Scrap metal recycler</strong> — sensible for a broken machine if you can
            transport it. Expect a small payment or none.</li>
            <li><strong>Junk removal service</strong> — the most expensive and the least effort.
            They carry it out; you pay.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The working-or-broken question decides most of this, and it is worth being sure of the
            answer. A treadmill that will not start is often suffering from something cheap — a
            missing safety key, a tripped reset switch on the frame, or a blown fuse. Ten minutes
            with our <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill won't turn on guide</a>
            can turn scrap back into something you can sell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sell-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Selling a Used Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Secondhand treadmills sell steadily, because a used mid-range machine is one of the
            better-value purchases in home fitness. As a rough guide, a treadmill tends to sell for
            around half its original price at about two years old and around a third at three to
            five years, after which the price flattens because what remains is essentially the
            frame. Condition, brand and local demand move that a long way in both directions. Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used treadmill guide</a>
            sets out how buyers think about value, which is the useful perspective when you are
            setting a price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price from local listings, not from what you paid.</strong> Search your local
            marketplace for the same model, or the nearest equivalent, and see what similar machines
            are actually listed at — and how long they have been sitting there. A fair price sells
            in days; an optimistic one sits for weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Make it easy to say yes.</strong> Clean it, vacuum underneath, and if you have
            been looking after it, lubricate the deck and check tension and tracking so it runs
            smoothly when a buyer tries it. Photograph it in good light, including the console
            switched on. List the model number, the deck size, the motor rating, the maximum
            incline and roughly how many hours it has done — many consoles show this in a
            maintenance or information screen. Mention whether it folds and whether you still have
            the manual and the hex key.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Be clear about collection.</strong> State in the listing that the buyer
            collects, that it will need two people and a van or a large vehicle, and whether there
            are stairs. This filters out the buyers who would arrive alone in a hatchback. Serious
            buyers will often work through a checklist much like our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill buying checklist</a>,
            so expect them to want to see it running, at speed and at incline.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Be honest about faults.</strong> A machine with a known problem still sells, at
            a price that reflects it. A buyer who discovers an undisclosed fault on the doorstep
            walks away, and you have lost an afternoon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="give-it-away" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Giving It Away</h2>
<p>
            If the machine works but the likely sale price is small — an older budget model, a
            walking pad with a tired belt — listing it free is often the more rational choice. It
            removes the haggling, it goes quickly, and the person who takes it does the carrying.
          </p>
          <p>
            Free-stuff groups, neighbourhood forums and the free section of local marketplaces all
            work for this. Say clearly what condition it is in, that it must be collected, and
            whether you can help lift. Expect a few no-shows; it is the nature of free listings.
            Asking people to confirm a collection time on the day saves some wasted waiting.
          </p>
          <p>
            Schools, community centres, places of worship, youth clubs and local sports teams
            sometimes want equipment, though like charities they may have rules about liability and
            space. A short message with the model and condition is the polite way to ask.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="donate-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Donating a Treadmill to Charity</h2>
<p>
            Donating sounds like the obvious answer and frequently is not, for practical reasons
            rather than any reluctance on the charity's part. A treadmill is heavy, bulky, slow to
            sell, potentially a liability if it is faulty, and takes up floor space a thrift store
            would rather use for something that turns over faster. Many donation centres do not
            accept large fitness equipment for those reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            Policies are usually set by the local branch rather than nationally, so the same
            organisation may accept a treadmill in one town and decline it in the next. National
            thrift chains, furniture-focused charity shops and building-material reuse stores are
            all worth a call; so are smaller local charities, shelters and veterans' organisations,
            which sometimes want equipment for their own use rather than for resale.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Call before you load it.</strong> Ask whether they accept treadmills, whether it
            needs to be working, whether they will collect, and whether collection is free.
            Charities that offer pickup often restrict it to furniture-sized items in good
            condition, may need it on the ground floor, and may have a waiting list of a week or
            more. Turning up unannounced with a treadmill in the back of a car usually ends with
            driving it home again.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you itemise deductions, a donation to a qualifying charity may be tax deductible at
            its fair market value — which for a used treadmill is a secondhand price, not the
            original one. Get a receipt describing the item. The rules have conditions, so check
            with a tax adviser rather than assuming.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="retailer-haul-away" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Retailer Haul-Away When You Buy New</h2>
<p>
            If the reason you are getting rid of the old treadmill is that a new one is arriving,
            this is often the least effort of all. Some retailers and delivery services offer to
            remove the old machine when they deliver and install the new one, either included in a
            premium delivery package or as an add-on fee — typically somewhere from nothing to around
            $100, depending on the seller.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is not universal, and the details matter. Ask before you order: is old-equipment
            removal offered, what does it cost, does it have to be the same type of item, does it
            need to be disassembled, and does it have to be on the ground floor or ready at the
            door? Some services will only take a machine that has been unplugged and folded, or
            will not handle stairs at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are still choosing the replacement, it is worth factoring in. A retailer that
            removes the old machine as part of delivery has quietly saved you a junk removal fee,
            which is real money set against a slightly higher sticker price. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill buying guide</a>
            covers what else to look for in the purchase itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="municipal-pickup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Municipal Bulk Pickup and Drop-Off</h2>
<p>
            Most local authorities have some route for items too large for the bin: a scheduled
            bulk-item collection, an on-request pickup, or a drop-off at a transfer station or
            recycling centre. For a broken treadmill this is often the cheapest legal option, and
            sometimes free.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rules vary, so check your council or city waste website before putting anything on
            the curb. Common conditions include booking in advance, a per-item fee, a limit on how
            many large items per collection, a requirement that it be placed out only the evening
            before, and in some places separate handling for anything with a motor or electronics.
            Some areas treat the console and motor as electronic waste and want them separated;
            others take the whole machine as bulk metal.
          </p>
          <p>
            Leaving a treadmill out without a booked collection is usually treated as fly-tipping,
            and in many places it is fined. It also tends to sit there in the rain for a week,
            which is no good for anyone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="scrap-and-recycling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Scrap Metal and E-Waste Recycling</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill is mostly steel, and a scrap metal recycler will generally accept the frame.
            What you are paid depends on the local scrap price and how the yard grades mixed items;
            for a single treadmill, expect a small payment or simply free disposal rather than
            anything that pays for the fuel. Call ahead and ask whether they accept fitness
            equipment whole, or want it stripped.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of the machine divides into a few streams if you are dismantling it or the
            recycler asks you to:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The motor</strong> contains copper windings and is usually welcome at a
            scrap yard, sometimes graded separately from the frame.</li>
            <li><strong>The console and controller board</strong> are electronic waste. Many areas
            run e-waste drop-off points, and some electronics retailers accept small electronics
            for recycling.</li>
            <li><strong>Batteries</strong> in the console, a remote, or a heart-rate chest strap
            should be removed and taken to a battery recycling point rather than left in the
            machine.</li>
            <li><strong>The belt, plastics and deck</strong> are generally general waste, though
            some recycling centres accept rigid plastics.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Most people are better off not dismantling a treadmill for scrap unless they are
            comfortable doing it; the frame is awkward, the bolts are often stiff, and the motor
            hood houses mains wiring. If the recycler will take it whole, let them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="junk-removal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Junk Removal Services and What They Cost</h2>
<p>
            Junk removal companies will collect a treadmill from wherever it stands and carry it
            out, and for many people that is worth paying for — particularly for a heavy
            non-folding machine in a basement or an upstairs room.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pricing is usually based on the volume the item takes up in the truck, with a minimum
            charge, and sometimes extras for heavy items, stairs or disassembly. As a typical range,
            a single treadmill tends to cost somewhere around $75 to $250, with the higher end for
            large machines, difficult access, high-cost areas, or a same-day booking. Several items
            at once is usually better value than one, so it is worth grouping the treadmill with
            anything else you want gone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Get a quote in writing that states the total, ask whether stairs or disassembly are
            extra, and ask what they do with it. Reputable companies sort what they collect for
            donation, resale and recycling rather than sending everything to landfill, and many say
            so on their websites. If the machine works, mention it; some will price it lower if
            they can resell it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compare the quote against what you could get by selling. A working machine that would
            sell for $300 is an odd thing to pay $150 to have removed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="preparing-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Preparing It to Leave: Folding and Disassembly</h2>
<p>
            Whatever route you choose, the machine has to get out of the room, and preparing it
            properly makes that faster and safer for everyone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Unplug it at the wall and remove the safety key.</strong> Then lower the incline
            to zero if it has power, before unplugging — a deck left raised makes the machine more
            awkward to move.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fold it if it folds</strong>, and check the lock has engaged fully. If the
            latch is worn or the deck will not lock securely, do not move it folded; lay it flat
            and treat it as a non-folding machine. A strap around the folded deck and upright is a
            cheap safeguard.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Remove the console and uprights only if it helps.</strong> For a non-folding
            machine, or one going through a narrow door or round a tight stair turn, taking the
            console and uprights off following the reverse of your manual's assembly steps reduces
            it to a lower, lighter base. Disconnect the console cable at its connector rather than
            pulling the wire. If you are selling or donating, bag and label every bolt and tape the
            bag to the machine, because a buyer cannot reassemble it without them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not open the motor hood</strong> unless the machine is going for scrap and you
            know what you are doing. Nothing inside needs removing to move it. The full lifting and
            stairs technique, including typical weights and how to measure the route, is in our
            guide on <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to move a treadmill</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wipe-the-console" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Wipe the Console and Your Accounts</h2>
<p>
            A connected treadmill is, for these purposes, a computer, and it may be holding more of
            you than you realise: an account login, workout history, saved user profiles with
            weights and ages, your home Wi-Fi credentials, and paired Bluetooth devices. Clear it
            before the machine leaves, whichever route it is taking.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Log out of the fitness platform.</strong> If the console is signed in to an
            account such as iFIT or another manufacturer's app, sign out on the machine and then, in
            the app or on the website, remove the device from your account if that option exists.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cancel or transfer the subscription separately.</strong> Selling or scrapping the
            treadmill does not cancel a membership; it keeps renewing until you cancel it in your
            account. If the buyer wants to keep using the platform, they should set up their own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Factory reset the console</strong>, if it has the option. Many touchscreen
            consoles do, usually somewhere in a settings or maintenance menu. The path differs by
            model and software version, so follow the manual or the manufacturer's support pages
            rather than guessing. A reset should clear user profiles and network settings; check
            afterwards that it has.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Forget the treadmill on your own devices</strong> — unpair it from your phone,
            watch and heart-rate strap — and remove it from any third-party apps you had linked. It
            takes two minutes and avoids a stranger's workouts appearing in your history.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="curb-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Safety: Kids, Curbs and Collection Day</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill waiting for collection is still a heavy machine with moving parts and a
            folding deck, and it deserves a minute's thought.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the safety key.</strong> Remove it and keep it — or give it to the buyer in
            person — rather than leaving it in the console. A treadmill left in a garage, driveway or
            on the curb with the key in place is an invitation for children to play on it. Never
            leave one plugged in while it is waiting to go.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not leave a folded deck unsecured.</strong> A folded treadmill standing upright
            can tip, and a latch that has been knocked can release. If it is going to sit
            unattended, lay it flat or strap the deck closed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Label a broken machine clearly.</strong> If it does not work, or has an
            electrical fault, put a visible note on it saying so. It stops someone taking a
            dangerous machine from the curb assuming it is fine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lift with enough people.</strong> Most full-size treadmills need two capable
            people and stairs need three. If a buyer or volunteer turns up alone, it is reasonable
            to reschedule rather than help them hurt themselves on your staircase.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Should You Repair It Instead?</h2>
<p>
            Before disposing of a treadmill that has stopped working, it is worth one honest
            assessment of whether it is actually finished. A good mid-range or premium frame can
            outlast several belts, motors and boards, and plenty of machines are thrown away for
            faults that cost little to fix.
          </p>
          <p>
            The cheap end — a safety key, a tripped reset switch, a fuse, a belt that needs
            lubricating and re-tensioning — is worth doing on any machine. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            walks through the common faults in order. The expensive end, typically a controller
            board or a motor on an older budget machine, can cost a large share of a new treadmill
            fitted, and that is where replacing makes sense. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            sets out the arithmetic.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you are disposing of it because you are upgrading, it may be worth checking
            whether the timing works in your favour: our guide to the
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy a treadmill</a>
            covers when prices and delivery offers tend to be most generous.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">14. The Bottom Line on Getting Rid of a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If it works, sell it or give it away</strong>, priced from local listings and
            with the buyer collecting. That is usually the cheapest and fastest route, and it keeps
            a useful machine in use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are buying new, ask about haul-away</strong> before ordering. If it is
            broken, try municipal bulk pickup or a scrap recycler, and keep junk removal — typically
            $75 to $250 — for when the access or the effort makes it worthwhile.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Before it leaves</strong>, log out of your accounts and reset the console, cancel
            any subscription separately, remove the safety key, and lift it with enough people.
            Charities are worth a call, never an assumption.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">15. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you dispose of a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If it works, sell it or give it away to someone who will collect it. If you are buying a new one, ask the retailer about haul-away at delivery. If it is broken, use your municipal bulk-item pickup or transfer station, take it to a scrap metal recycler, or pay a junk removal service, which typically costs around $75 to $250. Log out of any accounts and reset the console first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you get rid of a treadmill for free?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                List it free on a local marketplace or free-stuff group with collection required — working treadmills usually go within days. Some municipalities collect bulk items free or accept them at a recycling centre, and a scrap metal yard will often take the frame at no charge. Some retailers include haul-away of an old machine when delivering a new one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will Goodwill or other charities take a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, but do not assume it. Policies are usually set by the local branch, and many donation centres decline large fitness equipment because of its size, slow resale and liability. Call first and ask whether they accept treadmills, whether it must work, and whether they collect.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to have a treadmill hauled away?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As a typical range, a junk removal service charges around $75 to $250 for a single treadmill, more for heavy machines, stairs, disassembly or same-day service. Retailer haul-away with a new purchase ranges from free to around $100. Municipal bulk pickup is often free or a small fee. Get a written total before booking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a treadmill out with the trash?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not with ordinary household trash. Most areas require a booked bulk-item collection or a drop-off at a transfer station, and some handle the motor and console separately as electronic waste. Leaving one on the curb without a booking is often treated as fly-tipping. Check your local waste service's rules.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I do before getting rid of a smart treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sign out of any connected fitness account on the console and remove the device from your account online, cancel the subscription separately, factory reset the console following the manual, and unpair it from your phone and other devices. Then unplug it and remove the safety key.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Getting Rid of a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these cost money or an afternoon, and one of them keeps costing you every month.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying to remove a machine that would sell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A working treadmill a few years old has a real secondhand value, often around a third of its original price at three to five years. Paying a junk removal service to take it away throws that away. Check local listings for the same model before booking anyone, and list it with collection by the buyer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning up at a charity unannounced</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many donation centres do not accept large fitness equipment, and policies vary by branch. Arriving with a treadmill in the car often ends with taking it home again. Call first, ask whether they take treadmills, whether it must work, and whether they collect.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the subscription and the console</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Getting rid of the treadmill does not cancel a fitness platform membership, which keeps renewing until you cancel it in your account. And a console left signed in, with saved profiles and Wi-Fi details, hands all of that to the next owner. Sign out, cancel and reset before it goes.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Scrapping a machine with a cheap fault</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Plenty of treadmills are thrown out for a missing safety key, a tripped reset switch on the frame or a blown fuse. Ten minutes of basic checks can turn scrap into a machine you can sell or keep. Rule out the free causes before declaring it dead.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

